@prefix : <http://example.org/orexis/sensing#> .
@prefix orexis: <http://example.org/orexis#> .
@prefix belief: <http://example.org/orexis/belief#> .
@prefix ssn: <http://www.w3.org/ns/ssn/> .
@prefix schema: <https://schema.org/> .
@prefix sosa: <http://www.w3.org/ns/sosa/> .
@prefix ssn-system: <http://www.w3.org/ns/ssn/systems/> .
@prefix owl: <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix sh: <http://www.w3.org/ns/shacl#> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

<> a orexis:OntologyGraph .    # this document is one graph, and this is what it is

#  OREXIS SENSING, AGENT 0.2.0.
#  The sensing layer of Agent 0.2.0 speaks SOSA and SSN, and declares only what they lack. A
#  sensor sosa:observes a property and sosa:isHostedBy what it is mounted in — the feature of
#  interest, or a sosa:Sample that sosa:isSampleOf one — its board reaches it by ssn:hasSubSystem,
#  and how often it reports is its ssn-system:Frequency; a range is SSN-System's, stated by the
#  host, by what the host is a sample of, or by the sensor itself. Declared here is the graph a
#  percept is kept in and the link from one percept to the one before it, how long a sensor may be
#  silent before it is doubted and the number one that is stuck keeps giving, the margin a range's
#  condition states for a domain's transition to hold a state by, and the pipeline's own concepts,
#  since neither standard says how
#  bytes become a number: a codec, the binding of a sensor to one, the member that ships, and the
#  pointer; and how a number becomes a quantity, which is a scaling and a calibration the world states,
#  and the rules. Sensing is the translation row of the agent stack and
#  nothing above it: a transport hands it bytes, it makes a number of them and writes one
#  sosa:Observation per reading — a PERCEPT, in a graph of its own, linked to the one before it,
#  holding until the next arrives or is due and a grace past it — keeps a sensor's last few, as deep
#  as its rules read, and on the container's tick it asks which sensors have gone silent,
#  a silence computed from the latest percept's end and never written. It predicts nothing — that is the prediction package's — and concludes
#  nothing itself: what a percept is of, its quantity and whether its sensor is stuck are revisions,
#  concluded by the rules this layer ships (rules.ttl beside this file) and run by the deliberator.
#  What the agent holds true of a subject is a domain's transition, triggered by the percept
#  arriving, and none of this layer's words (an-observation-is-a-percept-and-the-mind-reads-only-beliefs).
#  No word of any transport is spoken here.

#  THE ROLE THIS LAYER SERVES (knowledge/domain/sensing/observer.md): an agent that plays an
#  observer runs sensing's part, and the boot loads this layer for it and for nothing else. BENEATH
#  THE DELIBERATOR, because what a percept is of and its quantity are revisions: the rules this layer
#  ships conclude them and the deliberator runs those rules, so an observer that were no deliberator
#  would hold numbers that are of nothing. That is how this layer says what it needs of the belief
#  package without the runtime knowing it (a-package-is-loaded-only-for-a-role-the-agent-is-declared-in).
#
#  AN OBSERVER IS A ROLE THE AGENT PLAYS, AND IT CARRIES THE SENSORS IT READS
#  (a-role-is-an-instance-an-agent-plays): `:fern_agent orexis:plays [ a sensing:Observer ;
#  sensing:sensor :probe ]`. Which sensors report to an agent is DECLARED, where it was derived from
#  where they are mounted, so a probe moved into a pot the agent acts for is not its sensor until
#  someone says so. Onboarding holds the declaration to the world both ways: this shape refuses an
#  observer reading no sensor, and one reading a sensor mounted on nothing the agent acts for — not
#  on it, a sample of it, or a place containing it; `orexis-onboard` refuses a sensor mounted so that
#  no observer role of the agent reads it. And the role carries how far its sensors are trusted:
#  `silentAfter` and `stuckAfter`, the shape's `sh:defaultValue` where a role states none — the one
#  place the figure lives, read by `received`, `missed` and the stuck rule alike. A sensor judged by
#  another figure is read by another observer role.

:Observer a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf orexis:Role , belief:Deliberator ;
    rdfs:label "Observer" ;
    rdfs:comment """A role an agent plays that turns its sensors' numbers into observations: the role
    whose part is sensing's, which writes an observation per reading and says a sensor silent or
    stuck. Played, never derived, and carrying the sensors it reads (`sensor`) and how long it lets
    them be silent or stuck (`silentAfter`, `stuckAfter`).""" .

:sensor a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain :Observer ;
    rdfs:range sosa:Sensor ;
    rdfs:label "sensor" ;
    rdfs:comment """A sensor this observer role reads: what makes it the agent's, declared in the self
    graph where the role is played. What the transports subscribe to and fetch, and what onboarding
    grants the agent on the bus. It must report to the agent — be mounted on what the agent acts for,
    a sample of it, or a place containing it — and onboarding refuses one that does not.""" .

#  WHAT HOLDS OF A LIMIT NO OBSERVER ROLE STATES, read by `default_of` and by the stuck rule off this
#  ontology. The refusals of an observer are onboarding's (`requirements.ttl`).
[] a sh:NodeShape ;
    sh:targetClass :Observer ;
    sh:property [ sh:path :silentAfter ; sh:defaultValue 3 ] ,
                [ sh:path :stuckAfter ; sh:defaultValue 6 ] .

:ObservationGraph a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf orexis:Graph ;
    rdfs:label "Observation graph" ;
    rdfs:comment """One graph per reading, holding the sosa:Observation a sensor made — the number
    it gave, the instant, the sensor, the procedure, the instant the device says the result applies
    to where it says one, and the percept before it (`previous`) — from its instant until the next
    reading's, the latest until the next is due and a grace past it; and what the rules concluded of
    that reading, in its revision, which sensing has revised into this kind too, since what is
    concluded of what a sensor said is still what it said. A sensor's last `stuckAfter` are kept and
    older ones forgotten. Named for what it holds. A PERCEPT and no state, beneath `orexis:Graph`
    alone: no reader of the mind is handed one, and what the mind reads of it is the subject belief a
    domain's transition makes when it arrives. SENSING'S, and no kernel kind: no sensing, no
    observations, so the readers at the boundary — prediction above, a domain's command or saying —
    name it in sensing's word, and what lies beneath asks sensing (#944).""" .

:previous a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain sosa:Observation ;
    rdfs:range sosa:Observation ;
    rdfs:label "previous" ;
    rdfs:comment """The percept the same sensor made before this one: written by `received` into the
    new percept's graph as it arrives, so a sensor's percepts are a chain, newest first, and the
    latest is the one no percept names as its previous. Kept as deep as sensing's rules read — its
    last `stuckAfter` — so the oldest kept names one that is gone (knowledge/domain/sensing/observation.md).""" .

#  A FORECAST'S GRAPH IS THE KERNEL'S KIND, `orexis:ForecastGraph`, where sensing, prediction and execution meet (#992):
#  `received` writes one per stretch of a series, holding during it, forgotten with the rest of that sensor's forecast
#  when the next arrives — a belief and not a reading, so no prediction takes it for the observation in hand.

:Codec a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Codec" ;
    rdfs:comment """A wire format, both ways: how a sensor's bytes are a document and a document is
    bytes. The first stage of the pipeline, bytes → document → number, and a family
    in rule 2's sense: the members are interchangeable and a sensor is bound to one by
    :decodedBy. JSON ships with this layer; a member from a package declares its own term as an
    instance of this class and implements this layer's Codec contract, and genesis loads it by
    that term. Absent a binding, a sensor's bytes are JSON, which every board here speaks.""" .

:decodedBy a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain sosa:Sensor ;
    rdfs:range :Codec ;
    rdfs:label "decoded by" ;
    rdfs:comment "The codec a sensor's bytes are read with — derived onto the sensor at genesis from what its device states." .

:Json a :Codec ;
    rdfs:label "JSON" ;
    rdfs:comment "The document is JSON, and a value in it is found by the pointer." .

:TwoPointScaling a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Two-point scaling" ;
    rdfs:comment """A RESCALE: the unit changes. How a sensor's number — a probe's ADC count — is a
    quantity in its property's unit — a soil-moisture fraction: the straight line through two points,
    each what the sensor reads there and what that stands for, named by its label (dry, wet).
    Unclamped, so a count past a point is a quantity past its own, which a transition then judges, rather
    than a saturated one hiding a flooded or frozen probe. The world states one per instrument it
    rescales (`scales`), beside the sensor; this layer's rule reads it at layer 0, and a sensor no
    scaling scales gives its number as its quantity — a thermometer already reports degrees.""" .

:TwoPointCalibration a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Two-point calibration" ;
    rdfs:comment """A CORRECTION: the unit stays. How a quantity an instrument reports with an error
    is the true one — a thermometer reading 0.4 in ice water and 99.1 in boiling — the straight line
    through two points, each what the sensor reads there in its own unit and what is true. Stated by
    the world beside the sensor it corrects, and read by this layer's rule at layer 1, after any
    scaling, over the scaled quantity; a sensor no calibration corrects keeps its scaled quantity as
    its reading.""" .

:scales a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain :TwoPointScaling ;
    rdfs:range sosa:Sensor ;
    rdfs:label "scales" .

:corrects a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain :TwoPointCalibration ;
    rdfs:range sosa:Sensor ;
    rdfs:label "corrects" .

:Point a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Point" ;
    rdfs:comment """One reference of a scaling or a calibration: what the sensor reads there (`reads`)
    and what that stands for (`standsFor`), named by its rdfs:label — dry, wet, ice — for whoever
    measures it again.""" .

:point a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:range :Point ;
    rdfs:label "point" .

:reads a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain :Point ;
    rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "reads" ;
    rdfs:comment "What the sensor gives at a point: its raw number for a scaling, its scaled quantity for a calibration." .

:standsFor a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain :Point ;
    rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "stands for" ;
    rdfs:comment "What a point's reading is: the quantity for a scaling, the true quantity for a calibration." .

:scaledResult a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain sosa:Observation ;
    rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "scaled result" ;
    rdfs:comment """The quantity in the property's unit, before any calibration corrects it: concluded
    at layer 0 from the number the sensor gave, through the sensor's scaling or as it is, and read at
    layer 1 to conclude the reading (sosa:hasSimpleResult). What a calibration's points read.""" .

:rawResult a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain sosa:Observation ;
    rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "raw result" ;
    rdfs:comment """The number a sensor gave, as the pointer found it in the bytes — all `received`
    writes of an observation beside who made it, when, and the percept before it. What two readings
    are told apart by when a sensor is judged stuck (`stuckOn`). What it is an observation OF, and the
    quantity (sosa:hasSimpleResult), this layer's rules conclude from the topology and the scaling
    and calibration the world states of the sensor.""" .

:readingPointer a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain sosa:Sensor ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "reading pointer" ;
    rdfs:comment """Which value of a decoded document is this sensor's, as a JSON Pointer (RFC 6901).
    A fact about a document and not about a bus: two sensors on one board share a message and
    differ only here. The middle stage of the pipeline, document → raw value, and the one that
    is data rather than a member. Absent, the pointer is /value, which is what every
    single-property device here publishes. It finds a number, one reading taken as the message was
    sent, or an array of readings, each {"value": number, "age_s": seconds before the message},
    oldest first — a sentinel's count, one on a heartbeat and two on an alarm — and each is an
    observation of its own, an earlier one holding only until the next one's instant.""" .

:startsPointer a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain sosa:Sensor ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "starts pointer" ;
    rdfs:comment """Where a decoded document keeps the instants a SERIES' values are for, as a JSON
    Pointer to an array of them, each the START of the stretch its value holds over, the next
    instant its end. A sensor stating it reads a series, its reading pointer finding the array of
    values, and writes a forecast graph per stretch rather than one observation.""" .

:endsPointer a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain sosa:Sensor ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "ends pointer" ;
    rdfs:comment """As startsPointer, the instants each the END of the stretch its value holds over,
    the one before its start: what a service summing the hour up to a time says of rain.""" .

# TWO LIMITS AN OBSERVER ROLE STATES of the sensors it reads, each a count of the doubted sensor's
# cadences or readings: stated on the role where it is played, and the observer shape's
# `sh:defaultValue` where the role states none — the one place the figure lives, read by `received`,
# `missed` and the stuck rule alike. A sensor judged by another figure is read by another role.
:silentAfter a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain :Observer ;
    rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "silent after" ;
    rdfs:comment """How many of a sensor's cadences its reading may be missing before `missed` answers
    the sensor silent, since the instant its latest percept ended: computed from that period, the
    sensor's frequency and this, and never written, since its premises are stored and nothing in the
    mind reads it. Stated on the observer role that reads the sensor; where it states
    none, the observer shape's default, three.""" .

:stuckAfter a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain :Observer ;
    rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "stuck after" ;
    rdfs:comment """How many of a sensor's readings may give the same number before its sensor is said
    stuck (`stuckOn`) — a count of READINGS, not of time (#944): `received` keeps that many of a
    sensor's percepts and sensing's rule says the sensor stuck when every one kept gave one number, so
    a sensor that misses readings is said stuck later than one that never does. Stated on the observer
    role that reads the sensor; where it states none, the observer shape's default, six — twice the
    silence: a live instrument's count moves by a bit within a few readings even in still soil, and
    soil itself drifts within an hour, so six readings of one number are the signature of a frozen
    oscillator or a half-lost wire rather than of equilibrium.""" .

:stuckOn a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain sosa:Sensor ;
    rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "stuck on" ;
    rdfs:comment """The sensor keeps reporting one number, and this is it: each of its last
    `stuckAfter` readings — every percept of it sensing keeps — gave this rawResult. Concluded by
    sensing's rule (rules.ttl) as a revision of the sensor's latest percept, so it holds while that
    percept does: the first reading whose number differs, and a silence, end it. Named for the state,
    not for the reading that tipped it. The second doubt the agent holds about a reading, beside its
    age: a silent sensor has no present, a stuck one has a present not to be believed (#462).""" .

#  THE MARGIN (knowledge/domain/sensing/margin.md, #944): how far past a bound of an operating range a
#  reading must go to leave a state held. The world states it on a range's condition, beside the
#  bounds it widens, sized from the instrument's noise; a domain's transition, judging its subject's
#  state from an observation beside the state before, reads it. Sensing's word, since a range is
#  SSN-System's and the noise an instrument's, whatever state a domain names.

:margin a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain ssn-system:Condition ;
    rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "margin" ;
    rdfs:comment """How far past a bound of an operating range a value must go to leave the state the
    agent holds of its subject: a subject held under the floor stays there until its value reaches the
    floor and this, one held over the ceiling until its value falls to the ceiling less this, and a
    value coming from another state crosses at the bound itself. One number for both ends, stated by
    the world on the range's condition beside the bounds it widens and sized from the instrument's
    noise. Absent, nought, and the state is the bare comparison with the bounds. Read by a domain's
    transition and by nothing else. `orexis-onboard` refuses one that is no
    number, a negative one, and one of half the range's width or more.""" .

#################  What a world must state of what sensing reads  #################################
#
# Held at onboarding to every world, over the documents as an agent's boot reads them: a sensor that
# observes nothing or is mounted on nothing reports to no agent, and a range whose bounds cannot be
# compared judges no state. Each says what it is, and onboarding refuses a world any of them reports.

